  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM MDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nH5 ridging begins to build across the southern Rockies today,\nreintroducing heat to the region. Temperatures today will reach\nthe 90s for the lower elevations, with warmest readings in the \nusual lower elevations of the Bighorn Basin and along the I-25 \ncorridor. The ridge axis remains to the east, allowing the \nmonsoon moisture to continue to draw northward into western WY. \nDaytime heating will allow another round of showers and \nthunderstorms to build along the western mountain ranges. A lack\nof a considerable shortwave sweeping through will keep any \nsignificant organization of storms from occurring, with showers \nremaining more isolated in nature. The lighter steering flow \naround the ridge will allow showers to move off to the northeast\nfrom mountain ranges, but once they detach from the range, they\nwill generally decrease and dissipate. With slightly lower \nmoisture levels and enough steering flow to move any storms, \nwill not need to continue any Flash Flood Watches for the day \ntoday. Once daytime heating decreases, showers will quickly \ndissipate, with mostly clear skies overnight.\n\nSaturday will see the heat ridge strengthen slightly, bringing\nhighs well into the 90s, with readings above 100 for the Bighorn\nBasin and Johnson County. There is a very weak shortwave noted\nin models, which may be enough to bring a little more\norganization to western showers and thunderstorms, but will they\nbe able to overcome capping from the increased ridging is the\nquestion. Right now, shower activity looks to be more\nwidespread, but showers would be smaller in nature, and would\nhave better motion to keep from sitting in one place. \n\nLonger range models don't have much change in this pattern for\nthe upcoming week. High temperatures hover in the 90s each\nafternoon, while the chance for monsoon showers continues for\nthe western mountains. Shower activity would be more dependent\non any passing shortwaves enhancing any lift.",
